WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.160 --> 00:00:04.620
THE STATE OF COCA COLA:&nbsp;

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:24.360
Coca Cola, one of the most recognizable brands in&nbsp;
the entire world. Likely even more recognizable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:30.180
than all of the tech giants. Given their global&nbsp;
omnipresence and instant recognizability,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:36.240
it’s no wonder why Coca Cola is so successful,&nbsp;
and why it’s one of Warren Buffet’s most famous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:43.620
investments of all time. But, eversince the turn&nbsp;
of the century, Coca Cola hasn’t actually been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:50.280
doing all that well. For example, between 2012&nbsp;
and 2020, Coca Cola’s annual revenue decreased&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.280 --> 00:00:57.840
by a third from $48 billion to $33 billion. Since&nbsp;
then, their revenue has recovered back above $40&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:03.060
billion, but this isn’t all that impressive&nbsp;
when we take a look at their main competitor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:10.080
Pepsi. Not only did Pepsi never have a Coca Cola&nbsp;
type decline, but they’ve actually increased their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:17.040
revenue from $66 billion to $83 billion. This&nbsp;
disparity becomes even more clear when we take&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:23.400
a look at Coca Cola’s stock. From their dotcom&nbsp;
peak, Coca Cola stock has grown 52% which sounds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:30.180
good until you factor in inflation and compare it&nbsp;
to the rest of the market. A 1998 dollar is worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:38.400
$1.83 today. In other words, to maintain their&nbsp;
real valuation, Coca Cola would’ve had to grow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:47.400
83%, but they only grew 52%. This means that their&nbsp;
real valuation actually shrunk by about 30%. Not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:54.000
to mention, the S&amp;P 500 has grown 200% during&nbsp;
the same time period meaning that Coca Cola has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.000 --> 00:02:00.120
lagged the overall market by a significant margin.&nbsp;
Now, you might be saying that much of the market’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:05.280
growth has been fueled by tech stocks, and that&nbsp;
background companies like Coca Cola didn’t enjoy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:10.500
nearly as much growth. And while I’m sure there&nbsp;
are background companies that have failed to grow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:16.800
Coca Cola’s main competitor, Pepsi has actually&nbsp;
destroyed it. During the same time period, Pepsi&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.800 --> 00:02:23.400
grew a whopping 339%. Pepsi hasn’t just outshined&nbsp;
Coca Cola within just the past 20 years either.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:29.880
Their returns over the past 40 years also beat&nbsp;
out Coca Cola substantially. In the past 40 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:37.440
Coca Cola is up 5700% while Pepsi is up&nbsp;
9000%. Pepsi has actually grown so much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:42.420
that they’ve almost overtaken Coca Cola&nbsp;
in terms of market cap. At this point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.420 --> 00:02:48.960
it seems like Pepsi overtaking Coca Cola is an&nbsp;
inevitability. So, how did Coca Cola go from being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:55.080
the undisputed king to severely underperforming&nbsp;
the market and nearly losing the crown to Pepsi.

00:02:56.540 --> 00:03:02.340
ROBERTO GOIZUETA:
In most cases, such declines&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.340 --> 00:03:08.220
can’t be linked back to a single factor. There’s&nbsp;
usually a broad set of simultaneous events that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.220 --> 00:03:15.180
slowly eat away at a company. But, that’s not&nbsp;
the case with Coca Cola. In fact, the entirety&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:21.000
of Coca Cola’s growth and their subsequent lack&nbsp;
of growth can be explained by a singular factor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:28.980
It’s actually a singular person: Roberto Goizueta.&nbsp;
Most of you have probably never heard of this man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:34.320
but this guy is single handedly responsible&nbsp;
for making Coca Cola into who they are today.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:40.620
And if you don’t believe me, just take a look&nbsp;
at this. Roberto became the chairman and CEO&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:48.000
of Coca Cola in 1981. At the time, Coca Cola had&nbsp;
hit what seemed like a market peak. For 14 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:53.520
the company hadn’t moved an inch. And this&nbsp;
is terrible when you consider the rampant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:59.220
inflation throughout the 1970s. So, not much&nbsp;
was happening when Roberto took over. Similarly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.940 --> 00:04:06.120
not much has happened since Roberto left. Roberto&nbsp;
served as CEO up until his death in late 1997,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:12.180
and as we discussed at the beginning, this is how&nbsp;
Coca Cola stock has performed ever since. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:18.300
here’s the magic, if we zoom out, you’ll see&nbsp;
that Coca Cola went on a monstrous run between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:27.840
1982 and 1998 growing a whopping 7200%. In other&nbsp;
words, virtually all of Coca Cola’s growth within&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.840 --> 00:04:35.280
the past 60 years occurred 1 year after Roberto&nbsp;
took over to 1 year after he died. This is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:42.480
correlation, this is causation, so what exactly&nbsp;
did Roberto even do? Well, he did a lot of things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:48.000
but all his actions can be boiled down to three&nbsp;
main categories starting with knowing the company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:53.100
Have you ever wondered why companies lose their&nbsp;
soul after their founders and cofoudners leave?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:58.860
Well, it’s because it’s rare that anyone else in&nbsp;
the entire world knows the company as well as they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:05.280
do. So, once the founders leave, each attribute&nbsp;
of the company becomes more and more generic until&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:10.140
company becomes a soulless corporation.&nbsp;
Roberto wasn’t the founder of Coca Cola,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.140 --> 00:05:16.620
but he knew the company as well as any founder&nbsp;
could’ve as he started at the very bottom. Roberto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.620 --> 00:05:22.200
was a Cuban refugee who joined the company as an&nbsp;
entry level factory worker in his early 20s. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:28.080
he would stick with the company literally for the&nbsp;
rest of his life, so by the time he became CEO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:33.720
he knew the company like the back of his hand.&nbsp;
Not only did he know the company, but he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:39.180
willing to make massive bets, which brings us&nbsp;
into his second key attribute: risk taking.

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:45.000
BETTING BIG:
Before Roberto tookover,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.000 --> 00:05:50.940
Coca Cola was an extremely conservative company.&nbsp;
In fact, the company had never borrowed money&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:57.360
throughout their entire 100 year history. Roberto&nbsp;
threw that tradition completely out the window and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:02.340
borrowed billions to make massive international&nbsp;
bets. He spent a lot of this money on creating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.340 --> 00:06:07.260
international vertical integration. For&nbsp;
example, he spent $30 million to acquire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.260 --> 00:06:13.320
a stake in the company’s Philippine bottler. This&nbsp;
was especially risky given that this was not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:17.760
Coca Cola’s largest international investment,&nbsp;
but Pepsi had a two to one lead within the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:23.160
Philippines. But, at least this investment was&nbsp;
within Coca Cola’s wheelhouse. Many of Roberto’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.160 --> 00:06:28.860
investments had nothing to do with producing soda&nbsp;
itself, and likely the best example of this was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:35.580
his acquisition of Columbia pictures for $692&nbsp;
million. Not only did this allow Coca Cola to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.580 --> 00:06:39.540
integrate their products into Hollywood. But,&nbsp;
they were able to make a profit on the company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.540 --> 00:06:44.700
by releasing several blockbusters and then&nbsp;
flipping Columbia to Sony for $3.4 billion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:50.520
Another massive bet was moving into Eastern Europe&nbsp;
as soon as the Berlin wall fell. This was despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:56.400
the area’s terrible regulations, useless currency,&nbsp;
and poor infrastructure. But, this move turned out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:01.740
to be extremely lucrative long term move given&nbsp;
that Coca Cola would go on to outsell Pepsi in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:06.600
Europe within the next three years. Roberto&nbsp;
wasn’t just risky when it came to expansion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:12.300
investments, and integration either. Roberto made&nbsp;
massive bets with Coca Cola’s fundamental business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:18.600
itself. For example, as president, he convinced&nbsp;
leadership to switch from sugar to high fructose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:24.780
corn syrup which cost 40% less. The success of&nbsp;
this move is actually what got him a promotion to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.780 --> 00:07:33.120
CEO. Roberto’s gambles just became even bigger as&nbsp;
CEO though. One of his first bets was Diet Coke.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.120 --> 00:07:38.940
Much of Coca Cola’s leadership was weary of the&nbsp;
product because Roberto had not only skipped a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:44.700
bunch of studies and taste tests, but he wanted to&nbsp;
attach the flagship brand “Coke” to the product.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:51.420
Diet Coke turned out the be the most successful&nbsp;
consumer product launch of the entire decade. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.420 --> 00:07:56.880
isn’t to say that all of Roberto’s gambles played&nbsp;
out so well. One of his most infamous failures was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.880 --> 00:08:03.240
New Coke. If you’re not familiar with New Coke,&nbsp;
in 1985, Roberto literally replaced the formula of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.240 --> 00:08:10.200
their best selling product Coke to better compete&nbsp;
with Pepsi. This turned out to be a massive flop,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:16.800
and Roberto would end up reintroducing the&nbsp;
original formula within just 77 days. Ironically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.800 --> 00:08:21.840
even this turned out to be a positive for Coca&nbsp;
Cola as it simply showed people just how much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:26.700
they liked the original Coke. There’s even&nbsp;
conspiracies that New Coke was just a massive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:33.240
ploy to strengthen the original brand. I don’t&nbsp;
think the story quite goes that far, but it just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:39.060
goes to show how much of an innovator Roberto&nbsp;
really was. He liked to bet big, go all in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:45.780
and fail fast, almost like a tech CEO. Speaking of&nbsp;
tech CEOs, when Steve Jobs recruited Pepsi’s CEO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:50.460
John Sculley, to come work at Apple, he told&nbsp;
him “Do you want to sell sugar water for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:56.400
rest of your life or come with me and change the&nbsp;
world?” Well, unlike John Sculley, Roberto stuck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:02.460
to selling sugar water but he changed the world&nbsp;
anyway. And by the time he died, he would become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:08.060
the world’s first employee billionaire,&nbsp;
but Coca Cola has never been the same.

00:09:08.060 --> 00:09:13.740
NOT THE SAME:
Almost instantaneously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:18.180
Coca Cola would begin returning to their&nbsp;
conversative ways. They started cutting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:23.220
back on their product line and simplifying&nbsp;
as much as possible. For example, remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.220 --> 00:09:29.400
New Coke? Several years after the failure,&nbsp;
Roberto would relaunch New Coke as Coke II.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.400 --> 00:09:35.430
But, after Roberto’s death, Coca Cola would&nbsp;
cancel Coke II altogether. More recently, Coca&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.430 --> 00:09:41.760
Cola announced that they would killing a whopping&nbsp;
200 brands. This included brands like Tab, Coca&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:48.480
Cola Life, Diet Coke Feisty Cherry, and Sprite&nbsp;
Lymonade. Coca Cola has also been de vertically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:53.760
integrating the company through a refranchising&nbsp;
project. In the late 2000s, Coca Cola decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:58.680
that it would actually be better if they gave&nbsp;
up ownership of their bottling plants and let&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:04.260
local partners take care of that instead. This is&nbsp;
the main reason that Coca Cola’s net revenue has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.260 --> 00:10:09.240
been declining over the past decade. The idea&nbsp;
is that while they’ll make less net revenue,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:15.240
their profit margins will be higher. But looking&nbsp;
at their net margins, it doesn’t look like much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.240 --> 00:10:20.340
has changed over the past decade. Not only has&nbsp;
Coca Cola pulled back on their existing lineup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.340 --> 00:10:26.160
and vertical integration, but they’ve refused to&nbsp;
venture into new products. One massive acquisition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:31.500
that they missed out on was Gatorade. You know&nbsp;
who purchased Gatorade instead? It’s of course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:37.260
Pepsi. Coca Cola has also refused to expand&nbsp;
outside of beverages, and this is one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:42.420
main reasons that Pepsi has been crushing them.&nbsp;
Unlike Coca Cola, Pepsi is not solely dependent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.420 --> 00:10:48.060
on beverages. They’re more of a food company&nbsp;
and own everything from Doritos, Cheetos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:54.180
and Lays to Quaker Oats, Tropicana, and Starbucks&nbsp;
Coffee. Yes, those starbucks bottles that you see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.180 --> 00:10:59.520
at grocery stores are actually made by Pepsi.&nbsp;
While Pepsi has been buying out or partnering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:04.800
with everyone at the grocery store, Coca Cola has&nbsp;
become more and more isolated to their classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:11.340
beverages. This doesn’t necessarily have to be a&nbsp;
problem. Oftentimes it’s better to consolidate and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.340 --> 00:11:17.640
focus on your core business instead of expanding&nbsp;
to everything under the sun. In fact, this is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:22.320
strategy that Steve Jobs used save Apple after&nbsp;
Pepsi’s CEO, John Sculley drove it to the ground.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:28.920
There’s one key difference between Apple and Coca&nbsp;
Cola though. Apple was consolidating into a niche&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.920 --> 00:11:35.040
within an exponentially growing market. Coca Cola&nbsp;
on the other hand is consolidating into a globally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:41.160
declining market. It’s not that people no longer&nbsp;
like soda. Rather it’s simply that people are more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.160 --> 00:11:46.860
health conscious and are actively looking to avoid&nbsp;
sugary drinks. In fact, over the last 20 years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.860 --> 00:11:53.640
sales of traditional soda has plummeted by 25%&nbsp;
within the US. This marks the single largest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.640 --> 00:11:59.160
American dietary change within the last decade.&nbsp;
In fact, the sentiment against soft drinks has u&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.160 --> 00:12:05.580
turned so hard that bottled water has been able&nbsp;
to overtake soda. Fortunately, Coca Cola has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.580 --> 00:12:10.800
large presence within the bottled water industry&nbsp;
as well given that they own Dasani, Ciel, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.800 --> 00:12:18.000
Smartwater. But, water can only take them so far&nbsp;
given the massive decline of the soda industry.

00:12:18.800 --> 00:12:25.440
THE FUTURE OF COCA COLA:
In the end, whether your 10 or 70,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:31.980
one attribute that we all share is our familiarity&nbsp;
with Coca Cola. Our parents and grandparents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:31.980 --> 00:12:37.980
grew up drinking Coke and Sprite and so did we&nbsp;
regardless of where you’re from in the world. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.980 --> 00:12:43.860
despite Coca Cola’s international popularity, they&nbsp;
haven’t been doing all that well for quite some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.860 --> 00:12:48.960
time now. This might be confusing at first&nbsp;
glance, but when you take a closer look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:55.260
Coca Cola’s history, it all makes sense. Much&nbsp;
of their international growth and dominance is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:55.260 --> 00:13:01.680
due to one crazy business man, Roberto Goizueta.&nbsp;
He’s literally responsible for all of Coca Cola’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:07.740
growth within the past 60 years. After his death,&nbsp;
however, Coca Cola simply returned to its old ways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:13.380
of conservative stagnation. And given that the&nbsp;
overall soda market itself is in a downturn, so is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.380 --> 00:13:20.100
Coca Cola. They’re by no means gonna go bankrupt,&nbsp;
but it seems like their peak is way behind us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.100 --> 00:13:26.940
and all that’s left is a slow painful decline&nbsp;
unless of course they find another Roberto. Did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.940 --> 00:13:33.300
you realize how much better Pepsi has performed?&nbsp;
